Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/3] nfc: add STMicroelectronics ST21NFCD support

The Fairphone 4 has an ST21NFCD NFC controller that nothing in mainline
drives, so NFC is listed as unsupported for the device. This adds a driver
for it and wires it up on the FP4.

Sending as RFC because two constants in the driver were reverse-engineered
from one handset rather than read from a datasheet -- ST does not publish
one for this part -- and I would rather have them looked at by anyone with
better information than present them as settled:

  - 0x7e, the byte the controller returns on an I2C read when it has
    nothing to send (the idle indication);
  - 0x90, the proprietary RF protocol number it reports for MIFARE
    Classic, which the NCI core drops as unmappable without the
    get_rfprotocol hook.

The part speaks plain NCI over I2C with no link layer, which is what makes
it a separate driver from st-nci (ST21NFCB/C, NDLC-wrapped) rather than an
addition to it. The transport is correspondingly thin.

Tested on a Fairphone 4: NCI 2.0 init completes, RF discovery runs, and a
MIFARE Classic 4K card is detected and reported to userspace with its
SENS_RES/SEL_RES and UID. Not yet exercised: card emulation, tag types
beyond ISO 14443-A, and suspend/resume -- feedback on what a reviewer would
want covered before a non-RFC posting is welcome.

Patch 1 is the binding, patch 2 the driver, patch 3 the FP4 device tree.
The DT patch depends on the driver's compatible and is included for
context; it would go via the qcom/arm-soc tree once the binding is settled.


